📘 Adaptive Vehicle Speed Control System – Documentation
1. Introduction
With the rapid increase in vehicles on roads, maintaining speed limits in restricted zones such as schools, hospitals, and residential areas has become a major challenge. Manual speed control often leads to accidents due to negligence and human error.
This project aims to develop an Adaptive Vehicle Speed Control System using ESP32 that automatically regulates vehicle speed based on zone detection and real-time monitoring, thereby improving road safety.

2. Objective of the Project
The main objectives of this project are:
To detect restricted zones using sensors
To monitor vehicle speed in real time
To automatically limit vehicle speed
To alert the driver when overspeeding occurs
To reduce accidents caused by human negligence

3. Problem Statement
In many accident-prone and restricted areas, drivers fail to follow speed limits due to lack of awareness or negligence. Traditional speed control methods depend entirely on manual control, which is unreliable.
There is a need for an automated system that can:
Detect zones automatically
Control speed without driver intervention
Provide real-time feedback

4. Proposed System
The proposed system uses sensor-based zone detection and speed monitoring to regulate vehicle speed automatically.

Key Features:
Automatic zone detection using IR sensors
Real-time speed calculation using Hall effect sensor
PWM-based motor control using ESP32
Visual and audio alerts
Embedded system-based implementation
When the vehicle enters a restricted zone, the system reduces speed automatically and alerts the driver.

